    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nhttps://github.com/cython/cython/blob/1f7e6ddaf16c7087a366dc9a9508b09c682fa0a3/Tools/rules.bzl#L80-L92\n\n**Background:**\nI am adapting a pre-existing cython build system to use bazel including cythonizing *.py files into *.so files.\n\nEverything was fine using the `pyx_library` rule target but I was getting ImportErrors when running my cythonized object files on my embedded target. Tracing it back, the issue was due to `libpython*` links being added to the generated *.so file.\n\n```\nroot@root:~$ ldd utils.cpython-311-x86_64-linux-gnu.so\n\tlinux-vdso.so.1 (0x00007fffdd0d5000)\n\tlibpython3.11.so.1.0 => not found\n\tlibc.so.6 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6 (0x00007f43a071f000)\n\t/lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2 (0x00007f43a0922000)\n```\n\nMy embedded target does not have a `libpython` that can be imported and my previous non-bazel generated *.so files did not have this hard linkage to `libpython`.\n\n**Issue:**\nAs far as I can tell, its due to the lines above (specifically Line 88); I can make a work around for now but it would be nice to explicitly control/override this hard linkage as its unnecessary for *.so files due to lazy resolution.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n```cython\n# Cythonize files into an .so binary\npyx_library(\n      name = name + \"_compiled\",\n      srcs = genrule_outputs,\n      cc_deps = cc_deps,       \n      py_deps = py_deps,\n      copts = [\n          \"-O3\",\n          \"-g0\",\n          \"-fomit-frame-pointer\", \n      ],\n  )\n```\n\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nI expect generated *.so objects to not have this `libpython` linkage.\n\n```\nroot@root:~$ ldd utils.cpython-311-x86_64-linux-gnu.so\n\tlinux-vdso.so.1 (0x00007fffdd0d5000)\n\tlibc.so.6 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6 (0x00007f43a071f000)\n\t/lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2 (0x00007f43a0922000)\n```\n\n### OS\n\nDebian Linux\n\n### Python version\n\n3.11.2\n\n### Cython version\n\n3.2.8\n\n### Additional context\n\nI am unsure if its possible to simply removing Line 88 to resolve the issue. My thought would be to have the single `pyx_library` wrapper function but instead have different target generation and dependency chaining whether `linkshared = 1` or `linkshared = 0`.\n\n*.so objects do not need the linkage to `libpython` while standalone executables do.",
